---
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';
const site = 'https://ewohub.com';
const slug = 'stripscore-cam-rank-explained';
---

<Base
  title="What Is Stripscore? Cam Rank Explained Across Chaturbate, Stripchat, BongaCams & LiveJasmin"
  description="Stripscore, cam rank, room rank — what these terms actually mean, whether every platform has one, and how the concept differs between Chaturbate, Stripchat, BongaCams, and LiveJasmin."
  lang="en"
  hreflang={[
    { lang: 'en', url: `${site}/${slug}` },
    { lang: 'de', url: `${site}/de/${slug}` },
    { lang: 'es', url: `${site}/es/${slug}` },
    { lang: 'ro', url: `${site}/ro/${slug}` },
    { lang: 'uk', url: `${site}/uk/${slug}` },
    { lang: 'ru', url: `${site}/ru/${slug}` },
  ]}
>
  <section class="art-hero">
    <div class="art-orb art-orb--1"></div>
    <div class="art-orb art-orb--2"></div>
    <div class="art-hero__inner container">
      <div class="art-meta">
        <span class="art-tag">Explainer</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="art-title">What Is <span class="art-grad">Stripscore?</span> Cam Rank Explained Across Platforms</h1>
      <p class="art-lead">
        "Stripscore" and "cam rank" get searched constantly, but the terms mean different things
        depending on who's using them and which platform they're talking about. Here's what they
        actually refer to, whether every cam platform has an equivalent, and where the terminology
        comes from.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="art-body container">
    <div class="art-layout">
      <aside class="art-toc">
        <p class="art-toc__title">Contents</p>
        <ol class="art-toc__list">
          <li><a href="#what-stripscore-is">What Stripscore Actually Is</a></li>
          <li><a href="#platform-cam-rank">Does Every Platform Have a Cam Rank?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#confusion">What Cam Rank Gets Confused With</a></li>
          <li><a href="#mechanics">Terminology vs. Mechanics</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
        </ol>
        <a href="#cta" class="art-toc__cta">Get help →</a>
      </aside>

      <main class="art-main">

        <section id="what-stripscore-is">
          <h2>What "Stripscore" Actually Is</h2>
          <div class="glass-card">
            <p>
              Stripscore is Stripchat's own name for the rolling popularity score it calculates per room.
              It's built from live signals — current viewer count, how long viewers stay, chat activity,
              follows, and tips — and it directly determines whether a room shows up in the high-traffic
              "Popular" category listing. It rises while a room performs well and decays quickly once a
              stream ends or engagement drops.
            </p>
            <p>
              Stripchat is the only major platform that gives this score a specific public name. That's
              part of why "stripscore" gets searched as its own term, while equivalent concepts on other
              platforms don't have a single household name.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="platform-cam-rank">
          <h2>Does Every Platform Have a "Cam Rank"?</h2>
          <p>
            Every major cam platform ranks rooms with some internal algorithm — that part is universal.
            What's not universal is the terminology. "Cam rank" isn't an official metric name on any
            platform; it's the informal, generic term people use to talk about "how visible is my room
            right now." Here's how the concept maps across the platforms we work with:
          </p>

          <div class="reason-card">
            <div class="reason-num">CB</div>
            <div class="reason-content">
              <h3>Chaturbate</h3>
              <p>
                Chaturbate doesn't publish a named score. Room placement in category listings is driven by
                a mix of current viewer count, tag relevance, and engagement signals, recalculated continuously.
                Models usually just call this "your ranking" or "room rank" — there's no branded term.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="reason-card">
            <div class="reason-num">SC</div>
            <div class="reason-content">
              <h3>Stripchat</h3>
              <p>
                Stripscore, as described above — the only platform with an explicitly named, publicly
                referenced score.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="reason-card">
            <div class="reason-num">BC</div>
            <div class="reason-content">
              <h3>BongaCams</h3>
              <p>
                BongaCams ranks rooms within categories using similar live-engagement signals, sometimes
                referred to informally as your "rating" inside model communities, though BongaCams itself
                doesn't publish a formal name or formula for it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="reason-card">
            <div class="reason-num">LJ</div>
            <div class="reason-content">
              <h3>LiveJasmin</h3>
              <p>
                LiveJasmin's room listing algorithm is proprietary and tied closely to its tiered payout
                system — visibility and earning tier are more intertwined here than on other platforms,
                but again, there's no single public name for the ranking mechanism itself.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="confusion">
          <h2>One Thing "Cam Rank" Sometimes Gets Confused With</h2>
          <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--warning">
            <p>
              Separately from platform-internal ranking, you'll also find third-party blogs and comparison
              sites that publish their own "cam site rankings" — lists ranking Chaturbate, Stripchat, and
              others against each other as platforms. That's a completely different thing from your room's
              individual visibility score on a given platform. If you're trying to improve your own viewer
              count, platform review roundups aren't the relevant "rank" — your room's live ranking signals are.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="mechanics">
          <h2>Why the Terminology Matters Less Than the Mechanics</h2>
          <div class="glass-card">
            <p>
              Whatever a given platform calls it, the underlying signals are largely the same: viewer count,
              retention, chat engagement, tip frequency, follower ratio, and streaming consistency. Learning
              to work with these signals transfers across platforms even though the label on the scoreboard
              changes. This is also why bot traffic and fake-viewer services fail everywhere — the mechanics
              they try to game are the same mechanics that detect them.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="cta">
          <div class="art-cta">
            <div class="art-cta__orb"></div>
            <div class="art-cta__inner">
              <span class="art-cta__badge">Go Deeper</span>
              <h2>Two guides cover the actual mechanics and the playbook in full</h2>
              <div class="art-cta__actions">
                <a href="/how-cam-algorithm-ranks-rooms" class="art-cta__btn--primary">How cam algorithms rank rooms →</a>
                <a href="/raise-your-room-ranking-score" class="art-cta__btn--ghost">The legitimate playbook →</a>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="faq" class="faq-section">
          <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Is stripscore a real, official Stripchat metric? <span class="faq-chevron">›</span></h3>
            <p>
              Yes — it's the term the model community uses for Stripchat's internal popularity score,
              which directly determines placement in the platform's Popular category listing.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Can I check my exact stripscore or cam rank number? <span class="faq-chevron">›</span></h3>
            <p>
              No platform publishes the raw number. What you can observe is your room's relative
              placement in category and Popular listings, which reflects the score without exposing it directly.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Does a service that claims to "boost your stripscore" actually work? <span class="faq-chevron">›</span></h3>
            <p>
              Fake-viewer and bot services can produce a temporary, detectable spike, but the score
              decays as soon as the paid traffic stops, and platforms actively flag the pattern. See our
              <a href="/raise-your-room-ranking-score">full breakdown of why this backfires →</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>Related Articles</h2>
          <div class="related-grid">
            <a href="/how-cam-algorithm-ranks-rooms" class="related-card">
              <span class="related-card__icon">📊</span>
              <span>How Cam Algorithms Rank Rooms</span>
            </a>
            <a href="/raise-your-room-ranking-score" class="related-card">
              <span class="related-card__icon">📈</span>
              <span>How to Raise Your Room's Ranking Score</span>
            </a>
            <a href="/stripchat_promo" class="related-card">
              <span class="related-card__icon">🔍</span>
              <span>Stripchat Traffic Bot Review</span>
            </a>
            <a href="/why-low-viewers-webcam" class="related-card">
              <span class="related-card__icon">👁</span>
              <span>Why Your Webcam Room Has Low Viewers</span>
            </a>
            <a href="/platforms-we-work-with" class="related-card">
              <span class="related-card__icon">🌐</span>
              <span>Platforms We Work With</span>
            </a>
            <a href="/stripchat-ai-model-recommendations" class="related-card">
              <span class="related-card__icon">🤖</span>
              <span>How Stripchat AI Model Recommendations Work</span>
            </a>
          </div>
        </section>

      </main>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
